// Elements. Damage and defense stats can be tagged with one:
//
//     stats {
//         damage 5
//         damage 2 element="fire"        // 7 damage in total, 2 of it fire
//         magic-defense 10 element="fire"
//     }
//
// `element=` works on `damage`, `defense` and the power and defense stats of
// kinds.kdl. Gems and reforges can use it too. Skills pick theirs per hit:
//
//     damage 10 kind="magic" scale=0.25 element="fire"
//
// That hit scales with magic-power plus magic-power tagged "fire"; other tagged
// power does not count. Formulas read the tagged part as self.magic_power.fire
// (self.magic_power is the untagged part).
//
// A melee hit is split by the weapon's damage: `damage 5` + `damage 2 element="fire"`
// is 5/7 neutral and 2/7 fire, each with its own multiplier.
//
// A target's element comes from its defense stats, weighted by amount.
// Untagged defense counts as neutral. All tagged defense still protects against
// everything; the element only changes the multiplier below. Take 10 fire and
// 10 untagged magic defense, hit by water: (1.25 + 1.0) / 2 = 1.125x.
//
// Mobs only have the stats of relics they are wearing (`/relic equip <id>`), so
// other mobs are neutral. Neutral attacks and neutral targets always deal 1x,
// whatever these say.
//
// default  Against a different element with no entry below.
// self     Against the same element with no entry below.
//
// name         Shown in stat names, like "Fire Damage". Their look comes from
//              formats/stats_format.kdl (`stat "damage" element="fire"` to change one).
// color        Color codes for that name and for the icon. Optional.
// icon         Shown on damage numbers of this element, before the number. An
//              icon from a resource pack font works too. Optional. A weapon
//              with several elements shows all of theirs.
// multipliers  Target element -> damage multiplier. Beats the defaults.

element-defaults default=1.0 self=0.7

element "fire" {
    name "Fire"
    color "&c"
    icon "☄"
    multipliers {
        "water" 1.25
    }
}

element "water" {
    name "Water"
    color "&9"
    icon "☂"
    multipliers {
        "fire" 1.25
    }
}
